511.3 CART TIER‑MODE SYNTHESIS
==============================
Domain-papers/500–599 Science
511 General principles of mathematics
511.3 Coordinate‑Free Algebra Relational Tensioner (CART)

CART = (C, K, D, C/K, H)

This document synthesizes the CART relational modes (R0–R4)
with the CART hierarchy tiers (Tier‑1, Tier‑2, Tier‑3),
producing a unified vertical structure of relational tension.


============================================================
0. PURPOSE OF SYNTHESIS
============================================================
Relational modes describe *how* a configuration is viewed.
Hierarchy tiers describe *how deep* its identity sits.

This synthesis binds them:

    Mode R0 → Tier‑0 (pre‑tier)
    Mode R1 → Tier‑0 (pre‑tier)
    Mode R2 → Tier‑0 (pre‑tier)
    Mode R3 → Tier‑1/2/3 (identity tier)
    Mode R4 → Tier‑1/2/3 (hierarchy tier)

Only identity and hierarchy modes (R3, R4) admit tier depth.
Raw, resolution, and defect modes (R0–R2) are pre‑tier states.


============================================================
1. RELATIONAL MODES (R0–R4)
============================================================

R0 Raw Mode      → C
R1 Resolution    → K
R2 Defect        → D
R3 Identity      → C/K
R4 Hierarchy     → H

Modes R0–R2 describe *tension evolution*.
Modes R3–R4 describe *identity depth*.


============================================================
2. HIERARCHY TIERS (T1–T3)
============================================================

Tier‑1: Shallow identity  
Tier‑2: Intermediate identity  
Tier‑3: Deep identity  

Tiers apply only to identity classes [x] and their hierarchical depth.


============================================================
3. MODE–TIER ALIGNMENT TABLE
============================================================

Mode   | Component | Description                     | Tier Applicability
-------+-----------+---------------------------------+--------------------
R0     | C         | raw relational field            | Tier‑0 (none)
R1     | K         | resolution / canonicalization   | Tier‑0 (none)
R2     | D         | residual tension measurement    | Tier‑0 (none)
R3     | C/K       | identity formation              | Tier‑1/2/3
R4     | H         | entanglement depth              | Tier‑1/2/3

Interpretation:
- Modes R0–R2 are *pre‑tier* relational states.
- Modes R3–R4 are *tiered* relational identities.


============================================================
4. SYNTHESIS: RELATIONAL EVOLUTION → TIER DEPTH
============================================================

The full relational evolution pipeline:

    R0 Raw
        ↓
    R1 Resolution
        ↓
    R2 Defect
        ↓
    R3 Identity
        ↓
    R4 Hierarchy

Tier depth attaches at R3 and R4:

    R3 Identity → Tier‑1/2/3
    R4 Hierarchy → Tier‑1/2/3 (same identity, deeper interpretation)

Thus:

    R0–R2: tension evolution
    R3–R4: identity depth


============================================================
5. TIER‑MODE SYNTHESIS MATRIX
============================================================

Mode | Tier | Meaning
-----+------+---------------------------------------------------------
R0   | 0    | Raw relational potential; no identity; no depth
R1   | 0    | Canonicalization in progress; identity not yet formed
R2   | 0    | Residual tension measured; identity not yet assigned
R3   | 1    | Shallow identity; low entanglement
R3   | 2    | Intermediate identity; moderate entanglement
R3   | 3    | Deep identity; high entanglement
R4   | 1    | Hierarchy confirms shallow identity depth
R4   | 2    | Hierarchy confirms intermediate identity depth
R4   | 3    | Hierarchy confirms deep identity depth

Key insight:
- R3 assigns identity.
- R4 interprets identity depth.


============================================================
6. TIER‑MODE FLOW DIAGRAM (ASCII)
============================================================

Raw → Resolution → Defect → Identity → Hierarchy
 |        |           |         |           |
R0        R1          R2        R3          R4
 |        |           |         |           |
Tier‑0    Tier‑0      Tier‑0    Tier‑1/2/3  Tier‑1/2/3

Identity formation (R3) is the “tier gate.”
Hierarchy (R4) is the “tier interpreter.”


============================================================
7. TIER‑MODE SEMANTIC SYNTHESIS
============================================================

Tier‑1 (Shallow)
----------------
- Identity minimally entangled.
- Few relational constraints.
- Low ultrametric depth.
- R3 and R4 both reflect shallow structure.

Tier‑2 (Intermediate)
---------------------
- Identity moderately entangled.
- Multiple relational constraints.
- Medium ultrametric depth.
- R3 and R4 reflect layered structure.

Tier‑3 (Deep)
-------------
- Identity highly entangled.
- Many relational constraints.
- High ultrametric depth.
- R3 and R4 reflect dense relational structure.


============================================================
8. ONE-LINE SYNTHESIS SUMMARY
============================================================
CART tier‑mode synthesis binds relational evolution (R0–R4) to identity
depth (Tier‑1/2/3), showing how raw tension becomes resolved identity and
how that identity acquires hierarchical entanglement.
